  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warm and muggy with temps in the 70s across ENC\nand some debris clouds from shower and thunderstorm activity \nwell to the west overspreading portions of the CWA this morning.\nThese debris clouds likely have some impact on rain chances \ntoday as well as high temps. Either way, still expecting it to \nbe hot and muggy across ENC for yet another day with highs in \nthe low to mid 90s inland and mid 80s across the OBX. Temps will\nbe slightly muted and how hot we get today will largely depend \non how long debris clouds from overnight activity hang around \nthe area this morning. For now, expect the area to be partly to \nmo cloudy this morning with some clearing this afternoon. \nCombined with dewpoints in the 70s expecting heat indices \nbetween 95-104F this afternoon. While this will be just below \nheat advisory criteria, it will still remain rather hot today. \n\nExpecting hot and humid conditions to continue into the end of\nthe workweek across ENC. Low level thicknesses will remain \naround 1430m and based off the offices climatological study, \nthis is forecast to result in highs in the mid to upper 90s \nacross the inland areas and upper 80s to low 90s along the OBX \nand coast Thurs/Fri before a front impacts the region Fri\nevening into Sat resulting in cooler temperatures this weekend.\nTemps may briefly warm up once again early next week as well. \n\nHeat indices are expected to peak around 105-110F Thurs and Fri\nwith heat headlines possible for much of the area both days. We\nencourage anyone planning on spending alot of time outdoors \nthis week to take proper precautions and stay hydrated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There have been some changes to the precip \nforecast over the next few days. While we still expect chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms each day across ENC into this \nweekend, chances look to have lowered a fair amount at least in \nthe near term (today/tomorrow) given the latest trends. While \nwe still expect several shortwaves to ride along the northern \nperiphery of an upper level ridge centered across the Gulf \nCoast this week, a mix of cloud cover today and some subsidence\naloft today and into the early part of tomorrow, may put a \ndamper on precip chances with any MCS's that ride along the \nsurface ridging remaining to our west today. Because of this \nhave only kept SChc PoPs along the sea breeze this afternoon as \na result. Have a slightly better chance tomorrow evening but \neven that has higher uncertainty than normal given it is highly\ndependent on if an MCS can track far enough east to bring \nprecip to ENC. Either way, with a moist and unstable airmass in \nplace, could see isolated seabreeze storms develop this \nafternoon and again on Thurs afternoon.\n\nA stronger northern stream shortwave is progged to push across \nthe Mid-Atlantic Friday with the attendant cold front pushing \ninto the area Fri evening into Sat bringing the best forcing \nand chances for widespread showers and storms across the area. \nHigh pressure build into the area over the weekend bringing \nbringing a drier airmass and minimal chances for storms.\nHowever, a second frontal boundary looks to approach next week\nwhich could once again bring increased chances for showers and\nstorms early next week.",
